FOUR QUESTIONS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. docs/AUDIT_02_STRUCTURE.md
carries the working; this is what it found and what this patch changes.

"ARE THERE TWO SIMILAR PAGES?" — MEASURABLY NO, AND I LOOKED HARD

Prose from all 58 public route templates, normalised, compared as 4-word
shingles. Highest overlap between any two pages: 12.6%, /guides/[slug] against
/services/[slug] — shared breadcrumb, CTA and next-step rail, not shared
content. No identical title except the notFound fallback. No identical meta
description. No pair of pages on this site tells the same story.

TREE-01 · BUT THE ARTICLE TREE HAD A PARENT THE MENU NEVER NAMED

/resources says "everything we publish, organised by what you are trying to
do". /technical-library says "the engineering reference behind our work". Those
are different jobs and both are worth having. But ArticleLayout.tsx:29 makes
/technical-library the breadcrumb parent of EVERY article, sitemap.ts gives it
priority 0.95, /resources links down to it while it links back to nothing — and
it appeared in no menu at all. A visitor following a breadcrumb up from an
article landed on a page the navigation could not show them the position of.

The Reference column now names both, with notes that say which is which. Same
fix as UI-NAV-02 made for /floor-studio and /design, for the same reason: the
labels alone read as duplicates and the notes do not.

VIS-02 · FIVE PAGES SHARED ONE SHARE CARD, AND THEY WERE THE FIVE THAT MATTER

verify:images passed and was right about what it checks: no slot declared,
bundled and sitemapped without a page drawing it. What it does not check is the
image a page hands to WhatsApp, iMessage, Slack, LinkedIn and every crawler
that reads Open Graph.

24 of 55 public routes declared openGraph.images. Most of the other 31 are
correct to inherit the default. Five were not, and each carried a full
openGraph block — title, description, type, url — with the images key simply
absent: the four commercial head terms and Floor Studio. Sharing the single
most valuable page on this site produced a card identical to sharing the
privacy policy. VIS-01 gave nine pages a card of their own and stopped one row
short of the ones that earn the most.

Five cards, drawn in the same flat schematic language as those nine — cream
#FAF7EF, the brand's dark, green and copper, subject in the left forty percent,
right third left empty because the platform lays its own title over it. One
idea each: five courses of plank with staggered end joints and the starting
course in copper; three abrasive passes coarse to fine and then one clean
finish bar; a four-step section with the treads picked out from the risers;
five board cross-sections showing five failures, all on one subfloor line; a
floor plane in perspective with chevron laid into it and a handle at each
corner.

Brace-depth checked on all five that `images` is a DIRECT child of openGraph
and not nested inside a sibling. That is the VIS-01a bug and it cost a build.

WHAT THE CAMERA AND AI ACTUALLY REACH

Chat on every page, ⌘K with the whole corpus on every page, Floor Studio at
depth 0 from header and footer. Native camera capture on phones only
(capture="environment" — a silent file picker on desktop). Live preview,
segmentation, depth and surface normals reach nobody, because none of it
exists. Recommendations always carry their reasons; the range always comes from
one price source. New York still gets Canadian dollars — GC-026, open.

Two things reach every visitor who uploads a photo and should not: AV-01 paints
hardwood over a person's leg, a light dog and a jute rug; AV-02 claims
`measured` at 72% error in a room with a rug. Neither is a reachability
problem, and neither is improved by more people finding the feature.

Verified: 66 guards pass. verify:images 162 slots, 162 on disk, 0 pending, 0
orphans. verify:navigation 58 public routes, all within 3 clicks, chrome 44, no
duplicate labels. lib/navigation.test.ts 198 assertions green. Orphans among
public routes: 3, all correct — /design/spec in-flow, /r a short link,
/verify-email an email flow.

